seg-0f1ffe21d381 = { "30" } days: { "96" } degraded cells in total, { "28" } of the { "30" } days with at least one, { "70" } incidents (clusters of touching degraded cells), no day with a day-wide effect. The median cell share of affected aircraft (the day's background) is { "0" } on { "29" } days and { "0.006" } on one, so the background guard (a cell must exceed the background by { "0.15" }) never changed a call in this region. The count of degraded cells per day runs from { "0" } to { "8" } (mean { "3.2" }). About a third of the published cells each day are <c1/> (too few aircraft sampled): coverage is the limit of this product, not the sky.

# text
seg-606aed861c95 = What this does not say: the days with more degraded cells are not "worse" in any physical sense, a persistent affected area would be understated (aircraft that fly only inside it all day have no baseline outside it and are not counted), and nothing here says who or what caused low accuracy.

